   139	### 5.5 MCP Tool Surface (agent-facing)
   140	
   141	Read tools:
   142	- `get_portfolio` (positions with live marks, distance to stop, open risk)
   143	- `get_account_health` (equity, settled cash, drawdown, token status)
   144	- `get_quotes(symbols)`, `get_price_history(symbol, range)`
   145	- `get_journal(filters)`, `get_setup_stats`
   146	- `get_risk_rules`, `evaluate_hypothetical_order(params)` (dry-run guardrails)
   147	
   148	Write tools (all create records; none touch Schwab directly):
   149	- `propose_order(params, thesis, setup)` → creates order_proposal, runs guardrails, returns evaluation
   150	- `annotate_trade(trade_id, post_mortem)`
   151	- `add_watchlist_item(symbol, trigger)`
   152	
   153	Explicitly absent from MCP: submit_order, cancel_order, edit_risk_rules, token operations. Submission happens only in the Rails approval UI (or a signed confirmation link), by the human.
   154	
   155	### 5.5a Agent Runtime (where Claude actually runs)
   156	
   157	Two runtimes, split by whether a human is present:
   158	
   159	- **Interactive (human present):** the Claude app (desktop/Cowork/web) with the Rails MCP server registered as a remote connector. Rails exposes MCP over HTTPS with bearer-token auth (token in dokku config, rotatable). This is where ad hoc analysis, screening, and order proposals happen, billed through the Claude subscription seat.
   160	- **Scheduled (unattended):** Solid Queue jobs on the VPS call the Anthropic Messages API directly with an API key, passing tool definitions that map to the same internal service layer the MCP server uses (in-process, no MCP hop). Output is rendered to email. This covers the 07:00 brief, 16:30 EOD, and Sunday review. **Unattended runs get read-only tools only.** These runs ingest untrusted text (transcripts, news, fetched pages), so `propose_order`, `add_watchlist_item`, and `annotate_trade` are not in their tool set; anything a brief wants to suggest is rendered as text in the email, and the human creates the proposal interactively. Write tools exist only in the interactive runtime. Each scheduled job also has a hard cap on tool turns. Rationale: unattended runs can't depend on a desktop app being open, and keeping the scheduler in Rails means the VPS is the single ops surface (one place for cron, retries, failure alerts, and the token-health gate).
   161	
   162	Cowork Scheduled Tasks could run the unattended loops instead, but that couples automation to a second scheduler outside the VPS and to the attached-folder pattern; not used here.
   163	
   164	One tool surface, two transports: the MCP server (remote, for the app) and the API tool definitions (in-process, for jobs) both delegate to the same service objects, so guardrails and read models cannot diverge between runtimes.
   165	
   166	### 5.6 Memory Layer (git repo, read by agent)
   167	
   168	Keep the file pattern from the original concept, but only for things that are genuinely human-authored strategy/context:
   169	
   170	- `investor-profile.md`, `strategy.md`, `watchlist.md`, `prompts.md`, `instructions.md`, `edge.md` (narrative layer over setup stats)
   171	- Removed from files because Rails owns them now: portfolio.csv, trades.csv, risk.md (risk rules live in the DB and are surfaced read-only to the agent).
   172	- Repo is git-versioned with one writer: John. Rails never commits to it. Generated context (setup stats, edge summary) is served through the `get_setup_stats` MCP tool and injected into scheduled-run prompts at runtime, so `edge.md` stays a human-authored narrative and there is no second writer to conflict with. The VPS pulls the repo on a schedule (or post-receive hook) for the agent's read access.
   173	
   174	### 5.7 Scheduled Workflows (agent, via Rails-triggered runs)
   175	
   176	- 07:00 ET pre-market brief: overnight moves on holdings/watchlist, gaps, today's earnings/econ events touching the book, stops at risk, settled-cash status. Delivered by email.
   177	- 16:30 ET end-of-day: fills recap, journal rows created, missing post-mortems flagged, equity snapshot.
   178	- Sunday edge review: per-setup expectancy over trailing sample, flag negative-expectancy setups, propose deactivation (human confirms).
   179	- Token-health nag when refresh token < 24h from expiry.
   180	

