 succeeded in 0ms:
   119	## What's deliberately not designed
   120	
   121	- No live-updating tickers or streaming charts. Numbers refresh on load and on Turbo-stream events (fills, sync completions). This is a decision system, not a terminal.
   122	- No P&L celebration/loss states. The journal treats +2R and −1R rows identically.
   123	
   124	## Reference direction: Mercury (web)
   125	
   126	Mercury patterns that map directly onto our screens, from the Mobbin screen set:
   127	
   128	- **Home → Dashboard.** Balance hero with a 30-day sparkline, accounts card to the right, a paired "money in / money out" block below, and a quick-action pill row under the greeting. Our mapping: equity + equity curve as the hero, positions as the side card, "open risk / settled cash" as the pair, and "pending proposals" as the first pill.
   129	- **Transaction detail slide-over → Proposal detail.** Amount hero at top, then a vertical timeline (source → destination with timestamps), then metadata fields, then notes/attachments/comments. Our order is the same: order hero, thesis, guardrail rows where Mercury has GL code and notes, timeline once approved. On desktop the proposal opens as a right-hand panel over the queue, as Mercury does; on mobile it is the full screen.
   130	- **Transactions table → Journal browser.** Summary strip (net change / in / out) above a dense table with Data Views, Filters, Date, Keywords, Amount as inline dropdowns, group-by rows with expand chevrons. Direct lift for journal: R this month / wins / losses strip, then trades with setup filter as the first dropdown.
   131	- **Payment confirmation → the deliberate confirm.** Mercury puts an authorization sentence directly above a single primary button ("By clicking Schedule Payment, I authorize…"). That is our Approve pattern: a plain-language line stating exactly what will be sent to Schwab, then one primary button. Given the stakes, keep a second gesture, but model it on this rather than a modal.
   132	- **"A similar payment has been created" callout → duplicate and warn states.** Mercury surfaces a possible duplicate inline in the form with the prior item embedded. Same component serves the wash-sale warn-acknowledge and the `submission_unknown` reconciliation notice.
   133	- **Status pills in rows** (Pending, Failed) → proposal states in the queue and `unprotected` on positions.
   134	- **Reimbursement approval modal** (document left, status timeline right, one cancel action) → the post-approval timeline state, with the thesis panel where the invoice preview sits.
   135	
   136	Not lifting: Mercury's left-rail IA (we have five top-level areas, not fourteen), the multi-account structure, and the light-purple accent. Reference screens are on Mobbin under the Mercury web app.
   137	
   138	## Reference direction, revised: Midday shell + Mercury detail
   139	
   140	- **Midday (web) → app shell and dashboard.** Icon-only collapsed left rail (~40px) with a top search bar; dark theme; chart cards with a headline number, legend (current / previous / average), dashed average line, and a timeframe dropdown plus "Customize" at the card row's top right. Layout: one full-width primary chart, then a 2-up row of smaller chart cards. Our mapping: equity curve as the primary chart with HWM as the dashed line; the 2-up row is open risk over time and drawdown from HWM. The rail holds our five areas: dashboard, proposals, journal, watchlist, system.
   141	- **Mercury (web) → detail panel, journal table, confirm pattern.** Unchanged from the section above: right-hand slide-over for proposal detail, transactions table for the journal, authorization-line-above-one-button for Approve, inline "similar item exists" callout for warn and reconciliation states.
   142	- **Copilot Money (web), supplemental.** Three cards worth lifting: "Transactions to review" with its reviewed-everything empty state (our proposal queue on the dashboard), the range-chip row (1W / 1M / 3M / YTD / 1Y / ALL) on the net-worth chart, and "Next two weeks" as a dated upcoming list (our earnings/econ events touching the book).
   143	- **Mobile supplements (iOS).** Revolut Business approval detail for the proposal screen structure; Navan's in-policy / out-of-policy idiom for pass/warn/fail rows and the override-with-reason sentence; Life Reset's Quick Add for the journal entry.
   144	
   145	Open interaction with the earlier question: a dark shell makes "halt mutes the whole palette" a weaker signal than on a light UI; the halt banner probably has to carry the state on its own.
   146	
   147	## Reference addendum: Origin (web)
   148	
   149	Origin adds two things neither Midday nor Mercury has, and it surfaces a screen missing from the inventory above.
   150	
   151	- **Benchmark overlay on the performance chart.** Legend row of portfolio vs S&P 500 vs component ETFs, each with its return, and a hover tooltip comparing "your portfolio" against the benchmark on a given date. Lift for the dashboard equity curve (portfolio vs SPY), and it doubles as the visual for the allocation module's correlation check.
   152	- **Holding detail page.** Price chart with range chips, a "Your position" strip (balance, shares, cost basis, return, allocation), then a right-column card with News / About tabs and a stats row below. This is our **Position detail** screen, which the inventory above omits. Our version of the strip: shares, cost basis, unrealized P&L, working stop, distance to stop, this position's share of open risk. Right column tabs become Thesis (from the originating proposal) and History (journal legs for this instrument).
   153	- **"Allocation & risk" card** with Asset allocation / Risk assessment tabs over a stacked bar: the allocation module's targets-vs-actual drift view, with the risk tab showing guardrail status.
   154	- **"Market recap" summary card**: the morning brief rendered in-app, not only emailed.
   155	- **"Pro view" toggle** on the holdings table: a dense-mode switch for the journal browser.
   156	- Small-caps eyebrow section labels (PORTFOLIO, HOLDINGS, ALLOCATION & RISK) as the sectioning device across cards.
   157	
   158	Origin ships both light and dark screens; the dark Reports screen earlier in this doc's search set is the same product, so it can inform either palette decision.
   159	
   160	**Monarch** is not on Mobbin. Worth screenshotting directly for the board: its transactions "needs review" queue and the net worth / holdings views are the closest consumer analogues to our proposal queue and dashboard.
   161	
   162	Screen inventory addition: **Position detail** (mobile + desktop), reached from any position row on the dashboard.
   163	
   164	## Reference addendum: Monarch (web, John's screenshots)
   165	
   166	Monarch resolves two open questions and contributes more directly usable components than any of the Mobbin sources.
   167	
   168	**Resolves the collar question.** The credit-score band: a horizontal track segmented at labeled breakpoints (300 / 580 / 670 / 740 / 800 / 850) with the current value as a marker and the filled portion in a single color. That is the collar: breakpoints are the method outputs (percent floor, ATR, swing, Chandelier), the filled band is the acceptable range, the marker is the proposed stop. Horizontal track wins over the mini price axis.
   169	
   170	**Guardrail headroom as budget bars.** Budget card rows (label, planned amount right-aligned, progress bar, spent left / remaining right in green). Direct lift for a "Risk budget" card on the dashboard: risk-per-trade used, daily loss used vs halt threshold, sector exposure vs cap, concurrent positions vs max. Remaining headroom in green; the bar goes to the warn color as it approaches the cap. This makes the guardrail engine visible before a proposal trips it.
   171	
   172	**Review modal → orphan-fill and incomplete-journal review.** "Is this merchant recurring? There are 9 more to review": entity card with recent transactions, a few form fields, and three actions (Skip / Not recurring / Save as recurring). Ours: "Is this fill part of a setup? N more to review", recent fills for the instrument, setup dropdown and planned stop, actions Skip / Not a trade / Save. The orange top banner with "Review now" is the pending-review entry point; the nav badge count (Recurring 10) is the proposals count on the rail.
   173	
   174	**Positions list, from the Accounts screen.** Per row: logo, name, sub-line, inline warning ("Account disconnected") where relevant, a small horizontal bar, a mini sparkline, amount, and a freshness stamp ("4 hours ago"). Ours: symbol, position size, inline "unprotected" warning, distance-to-stop bar, price sparkline, unrealized P&L, synced-at stamp. Collapsible group headers with group-level change and total serve the allocation view (positions grouped by target class).
   175	
   176	**Benchmark comparison row, from Investments.** Four equal cards (Your Portfolio | S&P 500 | US Stocks | US Bonds), each with period return and today, radio-toggle to overlay on the chart below. Better than Origin's legend for our purpose: portfolio vs SPY, with the allocation module's "diversifier" instruments as the toggleable extras. Holdings table grouped by asset class with Weight and a period-change pill is the allocation drift table.
   177	
   178	**Cash Flow analytics → journal analytics.** Segmented Monthly / Quarterly / Yearly, paired positive/negative bars per period with a net line and a dashed projection for the current period, then four stat tiles. Ours: winning R / losing R bars per period, expectancy line, tiles for net R, win rate, avg winner, avg loser. Skip the Sankey.
   179	
   180	**AI-origin marker.** Monarch marks AI-generated content with a sparkle glyph next to the title (Weekly Recap, Budget, Spending). Adopt it: every agent-authored element (thesis, brief, review suggestion) carries the glyph, so human vs agent origin is legible at a glance. Ties to the origin field on proposals.
   181	
   182	**Also worth keeping:** "Your Weekly Recap · date range" card as the in-app entry to the Sunday review; "this month vs last year" comparison line for equity vs prior period; Recurring's "in 1 day" countdown for upcoming earnings/econ events and "settles Tue".
   183	
   184	Palette note: Monarch's near-black surfaces with slightly lighter cards, orange accent, green/red only for signed deltas, is close to where Midday sits; the two are compatible as a single direction.

